{% set answerWhatIsIt %}
    <p>
        Temp Fast Mail is a free temporary email service. Open this page and you instantly get a disposable
        email address that receives messages in real time. There is no registration, no password and nothing
        to install. The whole project is open source, so anyone can read the code or run their own copy.
    </p>
{% endset %}

{% set answerHowItWorks %}
    <p>
        A temporary email address is a real inbox with a short life. Use it wherever a website asks for an
        email, then read the confirmation or verification message right here on this page. New messages show up
        automatically through a live connection, so you never need to refresh. When you are done, click
        Regenerate Email and you get a brand new address.
    </p>
{% endset %}

{% set answerRetention %}
    <p>
        Every inbox and every email is deleted automatically 48 hours after it was received. Nothing is archived
        and nothing is kept after that window. If you need something from a message, save it before the 48 hours
        run out.
    </p>
{% endset %}

{% set answerPrivacy %}
    <p>
        You can use the service without creating an account, so there is no name, password or real email to hand
        over. Addresses are random and unique, and all inboxes are wiped after 48 hours. The source code is public
        on <a href="https://github.com/kasteckis/tempfastmail" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>, so you
        can check exactly what the service does with your data instead of taking our word for it.
    </p>
{% endset %}

{% set answerWhenToUse %}
    <p>A disposable address is the right tool whenever an email is required but a relationship is not:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Forums, newsletters and downloads that demand an email before you can continue.</li>
        <li>Free trials and one-time offers you do not want tied to your main inbox.</li>
        <li>Testing your own app or website with fresh accounts.</li>
        <li>Shopping at a store you will probably never visit again.</li>
        <li>Keeping your real address out of databases that might leak one day.</li>
    </ul>
{% endset %}

{% set answerWhenNotToUse %}
    <p>
        Do not use a temporary address for anything you need to get back into later. Bank accounts, government
        services, your main social media profiles and anything with payments or password resets belong on an
        address you control for years, not for 48 hours.
    </p>
{% endset %}

{% set answerRejected %}
    <p>
        Some services block known disposable domains to cut down on fake sign-ups. If an address gets rejected,
        click Regenerate Email and try again with a new one. It also helps to complete the sign-up quickly, since
        the inbox only lives for 48 hours.
    </p>
{% endset %}

{% set answerSelfHost %}
    <p>
        Yes. Temp Fast Mail ships as a Docker image and receives mail through a free Cloudflare Email Worker, so
        you can run it on your own domain in a few minutes. The setup guide and the full source code are on
        <a href="https://github.com/kasteckis/tempfastmail" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.
    </p>
{% endset %}

{% set faqs = [
    {question: 'What is Temp Fast Mail?', answer: answerWhatIsIt},
    {question: 'How does a temporary email address work?', answer: answerHowItWorks},
    {question: 'How long are my emails kept?', answer: answerRetention},
    {question: 'Is Temp Fast Mail private and safe?', answer: answerPrivacy},
    {question: 'When should I use a disposable email address?', answer: answerWhenToUse},
    {question: 'When should I not use a temporary email?', answer: answerWhenNotToUse},
    {question: 'Why do some websites reject temporary email addresses?', answer: answerRejected},
    {question: 'Can I run Temp Fast Mail myself?', answer: answerSelfHost},
] %}

<section class="section pt-0">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="columns is-centered">
            <div class="column is-8">
                <h2 class="title is-3 has-text-centered">Frequently asked questions</h2>

                {% for faq in faqs %}
                    <h3 class="title is-4 mt-5">{{ faq.question }}</h3>
                    <div class="content">{{ faq.answer }}</div>
                {% endfor %}
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<script type="application/ld+json">{{ {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faqs|map(faq => {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: faq.question,
        acceptedAnswer: {'@type': 'Answer', text: faq.answer|trim},
    }),
}|json_encode(constant('JSON_HEX_TAG') b-or constant('JSON_UNESCAPED_SLASHES'))|raw }}</script>
